    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, he changed his tune on wanting the pardon before we released this report. And so, yeah, I think they felt the whirlwind was coming. Look, they knew what was going on and be quite frankly, speak frankly with you. I don't think Benny Thompson was fully aware of what was going on. He was a chairman of the committee, but Liz Cheney from all the appearances that we have from looking at all the documentation, looking at the transcribed interviews and the deposition, she was pretty much running the show. And also getting that from whistleblowers and people who were involved with the committee. And so I really think Benny Thompson was mostly a figurehead, but he was the chairman of the committee. And he was ultimately responsible. And he was the one who was supposed to be determining which documents were kept and which weren't. And they're playing a little semantics. Of course, Fox News had an article come out today where they had some extensive comments from Liz Janie and Benny Thompson who they're both saying, you know, it was extensively sourced information, and it was a very detailed report. And of course it was, but with only the information that you wanted to keep, right? I've always, my report to that has been, it's the, my interest is in the information that you didn't keep, not what you did keep, because we do know they cherry picked information. and they discarded information that did not support their narrative. But they're saying we kept all documents according to house rules, what they're saying they're using semantics. They made decisions. If you read between the lines, they're not saying they didn't get rid of data. They said they got they kept everything that they believed that they had to keep. So what they're saying is, we interpreted the rules to allow us to get rid of these documents.",
