    "turn_text": "right or in a great examples given by one of the attorneys uh... on our committee he said when you read a transcript and you read the words i shot the clerk that's totally different than I shot the clerk. I mean, you know, it's who me. And this is the thing that they can't get away from is they did destroy or at least did not preserve all the videotapes of all the depositions and transcribed interviews. They can't get around that. They can look, we recovered documents that were deleted from hard drives, over 10,000 of them. Most of them were digital copies of what we had. Right, they should have been preserved in digital format because then we can upload it to the database that we're using to do the research. By them deleting the digital copies, that makes it harder for us to do our job because then we have to rescan them and reenter them and get them into a database because you're talking of millions of pages of printed documents. I want to be able to go in there and query a particular word to find the connection points of what, you know, what they should have been doing. So I can understand that, that there are some documents that were deleted that are just copies. But there was a hundred and seventeen hundred twenty documents that were deleted and password protected that we could not get into and they were unique. We did not have any other documents electronically with those same file names. And so no one seems to remember what the password is and they were very professionally encrypted. So it's not like you can break into them. They're PDF documents and we've had our forensic teams look at it. But what is interesting about this is these were recovered from a hard draft. They have been deleted, but they were already password protected when they were deleted. So from my IT background and working in intelligence in the military, that tells me that they were password protected to keep other members of the committee out of them. They",
