  {
    "episode_id": 64860,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64860.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "So they were password protected before they were deleted, right? So that did raise our interest as to what those could be. I think that there are probably exhibits from some other interviews that we were able to recover. But what they did do, and this is something that a question has arisen recently, and we do have this in the report, is as we've talked about before, certain transcribed interviews and depositions were sent to the White House for archiving. What that means is once they go to the White House, so at the end of this Congress, step back from it, at the end of this Congress, we will be, every committee will submit to the clerk the documents that they want to archive from hearings and investigations and oversight, et cetera. So the chairman's responsible for that. The clerk then keeps them in the clerk's office for a time period. I think it's two years. And then they're given to the archives, the national archives to archive them as official government documents. They are house documents and the house can go back and request those at any time. However, if the White House sends documents to the archivist to be archived, they have executive privilege. so the house just can't go back and retrieve those documents alright so people need to understand that there is many reasons why they would have sent a house document to the the white house and I think the primary reason is so the White House would archive those. Same thing with Department of Homeland Security. They sent all of the transcribed interviews of the Secret Service agents. Those were sent to the Department of Homeland Security.",
