    "turn_text": "We have been unable to get those. I had the IG sitting in my office back a couple of months ago. And it was during, I think it was August recess, I flew to DC to meet with him that day. And I expected him to bring me those documents. Well, he shows up, he doesn't have them, because Secretary Mayorkas is refusing to release them. The issue here is he is an independent inspector general, which is not supposed to be subject to the decisions of the secretary. And the secretary would not release them. We kept hounding him and hounding him and hounding him until they said you can come to an in-camera review, which is basically we'll let you come to the Department of Homeland Security sit down and review the documents. So we did that, but they were heavily redacted. We were able to get some redactions that we could see them. And what we've discovered from what we were able to see, and we haven't been able to see the whole documents, is basically the redactions were covering up the massive failures within the Department of Homeland Security that day. So that is important to get those documents. And I think, you know, once Chrissy Noem comes in as Secretary of Homeland Security, she'll be able to dig in and get those documents for us. But we ran in the same issue with the Pentagon. We were investigating why there was a three and a half hour delay of deploying the National Guard to the Capitol. Now, this is the initial delays were because the Sergeant at Arms after the Capitol Police had requested. This was before the breach. He had made several requests for National Guard assistance, which had already been turned down by the Capitol Police Board. But once the shots were fired in the Capitol, then Speaker Pelosi said, yes, we need the National Guard. Get them over here. So the request was made to the Pentagon for the National Guard. They can't deploy the guard without a request because of separation of powers between the executive and legislative branch, right? The National Guard was readied. They were with their riot gear sitting at their armory that's less than two miles from the Capitol, ready to deploy. And the reason they were already there from early that morning is on January 3rd, President Donald Trump had delegated authority to deploy the national guard when he met with uh... general millie on january third knowing that there was going to be a lot of people at the cap",
