    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "And that's why we need to have a committee here of jurisdictions specifically for this because there's some other things internally in the capital that we've uncovered. We haven't been able to address some systemic problems related to Capitol Hill security. They're quite sensitive. And so none of the other organizations or agencies here in DC can address that problem. It requires Congress to address their own internal security. Um, but I have requested meetings with the speaker. Of course his time has taken up right now trying to fund the government, but to have a sit down conversation with him as far as what is the best way to organize this going forward. I don't think we should remain a subcommittee of a standing committee at this point because there's too many levels, too many hoops to jump through from a logistics standpoint. And we've had to fight some internal battles during all this too with internal efforts to circumvent the investigation. We had a time period when there was no speaker. which put a complete halt on our investigation because when this was set up, even though I was subcommittee of a full committee, on this particular topic, on the investigation, I reported directly to the speaker's office. And so we were put on hold. And so we need to have a clear definition. I believe a select committee is the best way to go. Select committee with clear jurisdiction that we continue looking into what really happened on January 6th, security failures in the Capitol, And some people are like, hey, you've uncovered enough, let's just move on. But if you think about what the FBI or the Department of Justice Inspector General just released",
