    "turn_text": "after negotiating with her attorneys with under the threat of a subpoena they finally gave us these text messages. So during these first two she is communicating with Alyssa Farah and tells Alyssa Farah that her attorney is telling her to tell the truth and you know he's representing her well. Then her and Liz start communicating directly over text and and Liz had already acknowledged that it was unethical to communicate with Cassidy who's already under have as an attorney representing her without that attorney right but she continues to do it So then in the third deposition, Cassidy then starts changing her stories a little bit or as she writes in her book, she started leaving breadcrumbs after she was talking with Liz Cheney. She admits to talking with Liz Cheney, she believes she leaves breadcrumbs for other questions. Now, Stefan Passantino, who is Cassidy's attorney, He testifies and he's told us, look, when I went for her third deposition, it was a lot different than the first two. There were Capitol Police everywhere. In fact, there were Capitol Police in the room with us, which had never happened before. And this is like during COVID, so there's nobody at the Capitol, right? So he's getting very suspicious. Well, she starts laying these bread crumbs and he doesn't intervene. He just lets her go and she starts changing her testimony. It was after that, okay, the third testimony, then Liz Cheney communicates with Cassidy and Cassidy says, Liz convinced her to fire her attorney and Liz finds her a new attorney, pro bono, right? All of this is, it's unethical at least. So they need a scapegoat though as to why Cassidy is suddenly changing her testimony. So Stefan becomes the scapegoat. And so they accuse him of telling her to lie. Alright, so this is all important because they actually ended up filing bar complaints against Stefan Passentino who lives in our district by the way. They filed bar complaints in Georgia and DC against him. I mean serious complaints that we're trying to get him disbarred it cost him most of all of his his clients because you know Politicized this whole thing was at the time But they tried to destroy his career to make him the scapegoat that he told Cassidy to to lie to start with so the way we found out about the videotapes is in february of twenty twenty three right after i started the investigation again call from staphon pessentino i never met before he says look i need the video of my third deposition with cassidy hutchinson because there's where the proof is never did what they said i did which was try to intervene and stop her from telling the stories or or uh... you'll get get physical with her he desperately wanted to find this video because it would have exonerated him that's when we found out not only did that video not exist none of them existed But Liz Cheney writes in her book of how important it was that they did videotape those. So America wouldn't know the truth, but no one seems to know where they are. So it was after that third testimony, they accused Stefan Passotino of telling her to lie in her first three. Then they call her back for another testimony, which no one else on the committee knew about except for Liz Cheney. and they had it in what's called Liz's Hideaway Office in the Capitol. This is where Cassidy came up with these extreme stories about Trump trying to attack the Secret Service agents, saying he wanted Mike Pence to be hung through cheeseburgers against the wall, all of these things. Instead of corroborating any of that, they call an emergency televised hearing, and they parade Cassidy in front of the entire world, and she starts telling these",
